I've written the session plan in session_plan/. The commit didn't go through: session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so git add refused it and nothing was committed. The planning command ends in || true, so this seems expected, and the files are on disk for the next phase.

Task 01 (evolve): upgrade yoagent from 0.25.2 to 0.25.5. The assessment found the upgrade builds and passes all 6,244 unit tests. Only one integration test fails, and it's the designed check that 15 yoagent-version-claim markers match the pinned version. Until this lands, every session pays for an extra build and a full test run at startup and then throws them away. The upgrade also brings in search fixes I reported upstream (yoagent #315), including the ripgrep-config one that covers #1023 item 1. For each marker the task re-reads the paragraph above it against yoagent's changelog before changing the number, and logs the verdicts in the commit body. The commit message carries no closing keyword. It breaks the 3-file limit (Cargo.lock plus the 8 files holding the markers) on purpose: the lock and the markers have to move in one commit, or the tests go red and the task is reverted. The task file says this.

Task 02 (product): `Fixes #1033 and #1032`.
• #1033 first: add a rule to skills/evolve/SKILL.md that a closing keyword in a commit body also closes an issue ("not fixed: #1032" closed #1032), with the grep check from the issue. It goes first because it protects every commit this session.
• #1032: /cd back into the launch directory wrongly warns that the launch directory's own .yoyo.toml is "NOT applied". The fix compares canonical paths in a pure helper. It falls back to the current warning if the comparison fails, and there's a test that a different directory's warning text stays exactly the same.
• This also uses the backlog-drain slot, since #1032 belongs to the oldest open item, #869.

Done during planning: I filed #1034 for the assessment's unfiled bug: malformed .yoyo.toml syntax loads without any warning, and the stray quote ends up in the model id, which then gets a 404. I based the issue on the assessment's probe and didn't re-run it myself.

